#: src/Admin/Pages/AboutPage.php:2147
msgid "<strong>Global</strong> — identical in every delivery of that submission, to every endpoint. Deduplicate by it when aggregating the same lead arriving via multiple endpoints."
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin/Pages/AboutPage.php:2148
msgid "The analytics conversion joined to the submission, and its session."
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin/Pages/AboutPage.php:2148
msgid "Shared between the frontend <code>form_success</code> event and the server-confirmed record, so the two detection paths can never double-count. Every Convermetry conversion report deduplicates by it."
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin/Pages/AboutPage.php:2149
msgid "One outbound webhook delivery."
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin/Pages/AboutPage.php:2149
msgid "<strong>Endpoint-specific</strong>; stable across every retry; echoed as the <code>Idempotency-Key</code> header. <strong>Receivers deduplicate by this alone.</strong>"
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin/Pages/AboutPage.php:2150
msgid "Supporting identifiers: a batch id plus each event's ordinal make tracker ingestion idempotent, <code>session_id</code> groups one visit, <code>completion_id</code> identifies one goal completion, and <code>lead_event_id</code> identifies one lead status change."
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin/Pages/AboutPage.php:2166
msgid "The two outbound message types"
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin/Pages/AboutPage.php:2168
msgid "Convermetry sends two kinds of webhook message. Every endpoint on the <strong>Webhooks</strong> page chooses which it receives, and the two are fully independent — an endpoint may take either one on its own, or both."
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin/Pages/AboutPage.php:2170
msgid "<strong>Analytics Reports</strong> — <code>message_type: analytics_report</code>. Scheduled, <em>aggregated</em> reporting for a time window, sent on the site-wide schedule you pick: hourly, twice daily, daily, or weekly. This is <em>not</em> one webhook per page view or click — an entire window is summarized into a single delivery. Each endpoint tracks its own delivery window, so a payload covers the time since <em>that</em> endpoint's last successful delivery, and a newly added endpoint can optionally be backfilled with the retained history. <strong>Send analytics test</strong> delivers one on demand."
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin/Pages/AboutPage.php:2171
msgid "<strong>Form Submissions</strong> — <code>message_type: form_submission</code>. One message per server-confirmed lead, delivered immediately through the background form-delivery queue instead of on a schedule. <strong>Send form test</strong> delivers one on demand."
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin/Pages/AboutPage.php:2177
msgid "<strong>For an analytics-only endpoint</strong>, check <strong>Analytics Reports</strong> and leave <strong>Form Submissions</strong> unchecked — no submitted form field values are ever sent to that endpoint. Analytics reports do still describe individual conversions (<code>conversions.recent[]</code>: conversion id, form name and ids, provider, and the visitor's IP when IP storage is on), so \"analytics-only\" means no field values, not no lead identifiers. The reverse works the same way. Both message types appear in the <strong>Activity Log</strong>, where you can tell them apart by their <code>message_type</code>."
msgstr ""

#: src/Admin/Pages/AboutPage.php:2178
msgid "<strong>Internal email notifications are a third, separate path</strong> with its own master switch and its own queue — see <a href=\"#notifications\">Notifications</a>. Notification sends do not appear in the Activity Log, which covers webhook deliveries only."
msgstr ""

